### t1-digits

For each input line integer n (|n| ≤ 10^15 fits in 64-bit). Print sum of decimal digits, space, reversed digits as integer keeping sign (unless 0).

Approach: convert n to string with sutlarai, handle sign. Sum digits. Reversed string, drop leading zeros, then convert back with plaltai, keep sign.

Edge: n=0 → "0" → sum 0, reversed "0" → 0 → "0 0". Good.

For negative: s = "-4500". Digits are chars '4','5','0','0'. Sum = 9. Reversed digits = "0054", drop leading zeros → "54", sign → -54. Output "9 -54".

Implementation:

```
plulai line := playadu();
edrushe line <> aiplai
    plulai n := plaltai(line);
    plulai s := sutlarai(n);   # e.g. "-4500" or "123" or "0"
    plulai neg := nonotru;
    plulai digits := "";
    atdoyal (s[1] == "-") {
        neg := eretde;
        shoyo i := 2 busale dra(s) + 1
            digits := digits + s[i];
        pla i
    } bulbayo {
        digits := s;
    }
    plulai sum := 0;
    shoyo i := 1 busale dra(digits) + 1
        plulai c := digits[i];
        sum := sum + plaltai(c);
    pla i
    # reverse digits, drop leading zeros
    plulai rev := "";
    plulai i := dra(digits);
    edrushe i >= 1
        rev := rev + digits[i];
        i := i - 1;
    aitudre
    # drop leading zeros: find first nonzero
    plulai j := 1;
    edrushe j < dra(rev) rulsai rev[j] == "0"
        j := j + 1;
    aitudre
    plulai trimmed := "";
    shoyo k := j busale dra(rev) + 1
        trimmed := trimmed + rev[k];
    pla k
    plulai val := plaltai(trimmed);
    atdoyal (neg rulsai val <> 0) {
        # print negative; careful: neg and val != 0
    }
```

Wait: for "-0"? Input won't have -0 presumably. n=0 gives "0". neg=nonotru, val=0 → print "0 0".

For negative with val: print sum, " ", "-", val. I can construct output string: `shaibe sutlarai(sum) + " " + ...`. For negative: `yaiyoyai sum; yaiyoyai " -"; shaibe val;` → prints "9 -54". Good.

Condition: `neg rulsai val <> 0` — precedence: comparisons bind tighter than rulsai, so `(neg) rulsai (val <> 0)`. Good.

But wait: for n like -4500, rev = "0054", trimmed="54", val=54. neg eretde, val<>0 → print "-54". Output "9 -54". Good.

Simplify: build string result.

```
plulai out := sutlarai(sum) + " ";
atdoyal (neg rulsai val <> 0) {
    out := out + "-" + sutlarai(val);
} bulbayo {
    out := out + sutlarai(val);
}
shaibe out;
```

### t1-floordiv

q = floor(a/b), r = a - b*q. Ailullai / rounds toward zero. For floor: if a,b same sign (or a%b==0), q = a/b; else q = a/b - 1.

```
q0 := a / b;
rem := a % b;
atdoyal (rem <> 0 rulsai (a < 0) <> (b < 0)) {
    q := q0 - 1;
} bulbayo {
    q := q0;
}
r := a - b * q;
```

Actually simpler: floor division: 
```
plulai q := a / b;
plulai r := a % b;
atdoyal (r <> 0 rulsai ((a < 0) <> (b < 0))) {
    q := q - 1;
    r := r + b;
}
```
Check: a=-7,b=2: q0=-3, r0=-1. Signs differ (a<0 true, b<0 false → true). r≠0 and differ → q=-4, r=-1+2=1. Output "-4 1". Correct.
a=7,b=-2: q0=-3, r0=1. a<0 false, b<0 true → differ. q=-4, r=1+(-2)=-1. Output "-4 -1". Correct.
a=7,b=2: q0=3, r0=1, signs same → q=3, r=1. Good.
a=-7,b=-2: q0=3, r0=-1, signs same (both neg) → q=3, r=-1. Check: floor(-7/-2)=floor(3.5)=3, r=-7-(-2*3)=-7+6=-1. Correct.

Need to parse two ints from a line: split by space. plaltai allows surrounding spaces! "converts a string of decimal digits (optionally with a leading `-`, surrounding spaces allowed)". So I could plaltai the whole line? No—"12 34" is not a valid number. Need to split.

Split helper: iterate chars, build list of tokens separated by spaces.

Let me write a tokenizer function:

```
nudro split(s) {
    plulai parts := [];
    plulai cur := "";
    plulai i := 1;
    edrushe i <= dra(s)
        plulai c := s[i];
        atdoyal (c == " ") {
            atdoyal (dra(cur) > 0) {
                sutse(parts, cur);
                cur := "";
            }
        } bulbayo {
            cur := cur + c;
        }
        i := i + 1;
    aitudre
    atdoyal (dra(cur) > 0) {
        sutse(parts, cur);
    }
    druna parts;
}
```

For floordiv, line has exactly "a b" with single space, so parts[1], parts[2].

