### t1-rotate

First line k. Then items (zero or more lines). Rotate right by k (negative = left). Print each item exactly.

If m items, effective shift = k mod m (right). k can be negative and up to 1e9. Compute r = k mod m in math sense: right shift by ((k % m) + m) % m using Ailullai's %? Ailullai % sign follows dividend: -7 % 3 = -1. So ((k % m) + m) % m gives non-negative for m>0. E.g., k=-7,m=3: k%m=-1, +3=2, %3=2 → right by 2 = left by 1. Rotating right by -7 ≡ right by 2 (mod 3). Correct.

Then print items[(m - shift + i) ... ] Let me index: result[i] = items[((i - shift - 1) mod m) + 1] for i 1..m. Easier: start = m - shift + 1 (1-based position of first output). If shift=0, start=m+1 which wraps to 1 (wrap-around indexing! index m+1 → wraps to 1). Nice, wrap-around indexing helps.

Print for i 0..m-1: items[start + i] with wrap. Using loop counter j from start to start+m-1, items[j] wraps automatically. 

```
plulai k := plaltai(playadu());
plulai items := [];
plulai line := playadu();
edrushe line <> aiplai
    sutse(items, line);
    line := playadu();
aitudre
plulai m := dra(items);
atdoyal (m > 0) {
    plulai shift := ((k % m) + m) % m;
    plulai start := m - shift + 1;
    shoyo j := start busale start + m
        shaibe items[j];
    pla j
}
```

Note busale with start + m: loop runs while j < start+m, so m iterations, j wraps via list indexing. 

### t1-weekday

Gregorian calendar, dates 0001-01-01..9999-12-31. Compute day of week.

