### t2-bignum

Add and multiply big numbers up to 100 digits. Implement with digit arrays (lists of ints). Read a and b as strings.

Addition: standard, from least significant. Store digits as list of ints with least significant first? Let's keep number as list of digit ints most-significant first, and process reversed.

Helper to convert string to list of int digits (MS first): loop chars, plaltai each char.

Add:

```
nudro bigadd(as, bs) {  # as, bs: lists of digits MS first; returns digit list
    plulai i := dra(as);
    plulai j := dra(bs);
    plulai carry := 0;
    plulai res := [];
    enala
        plulai da := 0;
        plulai db := 0;
        atdoyal (i >= 1) { da := as[i]; }
        atdoyal (j >= 1) { db := bs[j]; }
        plulai s := da + db + carry;
        # prepend: since building from LSB, we need to insert at front; lists only append... 
```

Prepending is awkward. Instead build result as LSB-first list then reverse at end, or store numbers LSB-first internally. Simpler: convert string to LSB-first digit list (digit 1 = least significant). Then add is easy appending. At the end, convert back to string by printing from end to start.

Let me define: toLSB(s): list where index 1 = least significant digit. Loop i from dra(s) down to 1, sutse digit.

bigadd(a, b): LSB lists. i=1..max(len). carry. Append s%10. Finally if carry>0 append carry. Return LSB list.

bigmul(a, b): result LSB list of zeros length la+lb. For each i in a, carry=0, for each j in b: t = res[i+j-1] + a[i]*b[j] + carry; res[i+j-1] = t%10; carry=t/10. After inner, propagate carry: k=j... Actually after finishing j loop, add carry to res[i+lb]: while carry>0: t = res[pos]+carry... Standard schoolbook.

Let me write carefully with LSB indexing (res index p = coefficient of 10^(p-1)).

```
nudro bigmul(a, b) {
    plulai la := dra(a);
    plulai lb := dra(b);
    plulai res := [];
    shoyo p := 1 busale la + lb + 1
        sutse(res, 0);
    pla p
    shoyo i := 1 busale la + 1
        plulai carry := 0;
        shoyo j := 1 busale lb + 1
            plulai p := i + j - 1;
            plulai t := res[p] + a[i] * b[j] + carry;
            res[p] := t % 10;
            carry := t / 10;
        pla j
        # propagate remaining carry
        plulai p := i + lb;
        edrushe carry > 0
            plulai t := res[p] + carry;
            res[p] := t % 10;
            carry := t / 10;
            p := p + 1;
        aitudre
    pla i
    # strip leading zeros (high end)
    plulai n := dra(res);
    edrushe n > 1 rulsai res[n] == 0
        n := n - 1;
    aitudre
    # build trimmed list
    plulai out := [];
    shoyo i := 1 busale n + 1
        sutse(out, res[i]);
    pla i
    druna out;
}
```

Wait — a or b could be "0" (LSB [0]). la+lb could over-allocate; stripping handles. Multiplication by zero: a=[0], b=[5] → res computed: i=1,j=1: t=0+0*5+0=0, res[1]=0. carry 0. Strip high zeros → [0]. Good.

But careful: carry propagation `edrushe carry > 0` — if carry becomes 0 it stops, good. But what if res[p] was 9 and carry adds making 10? t%10 fine, carry continues. Good.

Hmm but there's subtle issue: in carry propagation loop, when we set res[p] and increment p beyond allocated size? res has la+lb entries; max position needed is la+lb. i+lb max = la+lb. carry after last inner iteration placed at p=i+lb ≤ la+lb. If that position overflows (res[p]=t%10 with carry to p+1), p+1 could be la+lb+1 — out of allocated. But total product < 10^(la+lb), so highest coefficient at position la+lb, carry from position la+lb would exceed. In propagation at p=i+lb, if t>=10, carry goes to p+1. Can that happen? res[p] ≤ 9, carry ≤ ... carry from inner loop ≤ 9ish. Actually with standard algorithm total stays within la+lb digits, so final carry at p=la+lb yields t<10. For safety, allocated la+lb is exact max; the product of la-digit and lb-digit numbers has at most la+lb digits. The carry propagation writes positions i+lb which is ≤ la+lb, and result fits, so no overflow beyond. Fine.

To print LSB digit list as string: build from highest index down.

